deep.proof_idea:
One-day proof, from an environment with fcc.gov egress (this container's proxy blocks it):
1. Pull the last 90 days of grants for equipment classes DTS/NII/PCB/CBD via the EAS generic search or fccid.io date browse. Take roughly 2,000 grants that have public internal-photos exhibits.
2. Extract images with pdfimages and run a VLM (Claude) prompt: "list every legible IC top-marking, module label and FCC ID visible; note if RF shields cover components."
3. Measure four things:
   (a) % of grants with any legible radio-SoC or module identifier;
   (b) % where the primary wireless chip is identified via an embedded module FCC ID, a metadata-only baseline;
   (c) accuracy against about 50 devices with published iFixit/TechInsights/System Plus teardowns;
   (d) shield-coverage rate.
4. Kill criterion: if the vision step adds less than 15-20 points of radio-chip identification over the metadata-only module-FCC-ID baseline, the ML moat is thin.
5. Demand test in the same week: email 5 licensing heads (Sisvel, Via LA, Avanci, InterDigital, a litigation funder) with 20 sample unlicensed-implementer leads plus evidence packets, and ask for a paid pilot.
